.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs \- create a two-way ccol_channel with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel *ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_size,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a ccol_channel. Two circular queues hold it inside,
one for each direction: ccol_owner_to_workers and ccol_workers_to_owner.
The function creates both with the same max_size, so the two directions
have the same capacity.

The function takes the ID of the thread that calls it. That thread becomes
the owner of the ccol_channel. Every later ccol_chan_ call compares the ID
of its own thread against that ID, and it routes itself from the result.
The owner sends to ccol_owner_to_workers and receives from
ccol_workers_to_owner. Every other thread does the opposite. Those calls
need no direction argument.

The function allocates both queues below the channel, and the ccol_channel
struct itself, through mmgmt_procs. The ccol_channel keeps its own copy of
those procedures and frees itself through them. The ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of
the caller therefore does not need to live longer than this call. A NULL
value selects the default malloc and free, which is what the
ccol_channel_create() macro does.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size     Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold. One
             circular queue holds each direction. The bound of
             ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs(3) therefore applies:
             at least 1, no larger than ccol_max_elem_count, and no
             larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t).
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a pointer to the new ccol_channel. If
err_str is not NULL, the function sets *err_str to NULL.

On failure, the function gives NULL. If err_str is not NULL, *err_str then
describes the reason. There are three reasons for a failure. First,
mmgmt_procs is malformed, because it sets some but not all of malloc,
calloc, realloc and free. Second, an allocation failed. Third, the function
could not create one of the two circular queues below the channel, for any
of the reasons in ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs(3). A max_size
that is out of range is one of them. The function can create the first
queue and then fail on the second one. It then frees the first queue before
it returns. No failure path therefore leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe.
You must destroy the channel with ccol_channel_destroy() after you finish
with it.
The channel picks the direction itself from the ID of the thread that
calls it.
The owner thread is the thread that called this function. It must stay
alive for all the time that the ccol_channel is in use. To route a
message, the channel compares the ID of the thread that calls it against
the ID that it took here. After the first owner exits, the OS can give
the same ID to a new, unrelated thread. The channel then routes that
thread as the owner too, and it reports nothing.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with a ccol_channel. The
first way is to call fork(2) before you create the ccol_channel. The second
way is to create the ccol_channel, then call fork(2) and immediately call
exec(3). One pattern is not supported: a program continues to run with no
exec(3) in between while a ccol_channel from before the fork is still live.
On top of that rule, the library adds a defense in depth. The child never
inherits a lock in the locked state from another thread that held it at the
instant of the fork. Also, neither queue below the channel owns a thread of
its own. A ccol_channel that a child inherits in that way therefore stays
fully usable.
.fi
